**3.3 Information to Include in a Request.** To facilitate prompt processing, a request should include the following information:

(a) Name and contact information of the individual requesting the accommodation;
(b) The specific program, service, or facility to which access is sought;
(c) A description of the barrier or limitation encountered;
(d) The specific accommodation requested, if known;
(e) Any relevant documentation as described in Section 5 below.

The absence of any of the above information shall not, by itself, result in denial of a request. The DAO will engage in the interactive process to obtain necessary information.

**3.4 Interactive Process.** Upon receipt of a request, the DAO will initiate an interactive process with the individual to:

(a) Understand the nature of the individual's disability and the barriers they face;
(b) Identify potential accommodations that would be effective;
(c) Assess the feasibility and cost of proposed accommodations;
(d) Select and implement an appropriate accommodation.

The interactive process is intended to be collaborative and timely. The DAO will make reasonable efforts to respond to all requests within the timeframes set forth in Section 7.

---

**SECTION 4: ACCOMMODATION TYPES**

**4.1 Physical and Architectural Modifications.** Modifications to the physical environment, including but not limited to:

(a) Installation of ramps or lifts;
(b) Widening of doorways;
(c) Adjustment of counter heights;
(d) Repair or replacement of automatic door openers;
(e) Signage improvements, including Braille and tactile signage;
(f) Parking space designation and striping;
(g) Restroom modifications.

**4.2 Policy and Procedural Modifications.** Adjustments to District policies, practices, or procedures, including but not limited to:

Accessibility Inspection Form 2024 has_revision_date 2024-01-15 date. Record status: current.

(a) Extended time on examinations;
(b) Priority registration for classes;
(c) Modified attendance policies;
(d) Provision of materials in alternate formats;
(e) Permission to bring service animals;
(f) Reassignment of classes or activities to accessible locations.

**4.3 Auxiliary Aids and Services.** Services and devices that enable effective communication, including but not limited to:

(a) Qualified sign language interpreters;
(b) Real-time captioning (CART);
(c) Assistive listening devices;
(d) Braille materials;
(e) Large print materials;
(f) Screen-reading software and accessible electronic documents;
(g) Note-taking assistance.

**4.4 Temporary Accommodations.** In cases where an individual has a temporary disability or a temporary barrier exists, the DAO may authorize temporary accommodations such as:

(a) Temporary ramps;
(b) Relocation of classes or events;
(c) Provision of portable assistive technology;
(d) Temporary parking accommodations.

---

**SECTION 5: DOCUMENTATION REQUIREMENTS**

**5.1 General Rule.** Documentation is required only when the disability and/or the need for accommodation is not obvious. The DAO may request reasonable documentation that is:

(a) Sufficient to establish that the individual has a disability;
(b) Sufficient to describe the functional limitations caused by the disability;
(c) Sufficient to support the need for the specific accommodation requested.

**5.2 Acceptable Forms of Documentation.** Documentation may include, but is not limited to:

(a) A letter from a licensed physician, psychologist, or other qualified health care provider;
(b) A copy of an Individualized Education Program (IEP) or Section 504 Plan;
(c) A determination of disability from a state or federal agency;
(d) A previous accommodation plan from another educational institution;
(e) Medical records, diagnostic test results, or clinical notes.

**6.1 Establishment and Purpose.** The District Accessibility Office (DAO) maintains the Eligibility Review Committee (ERC) to review eligibility determinations, accommodation requests, and appeals in a consistent and timely manner. The ERC acts on behalf of the Superintendent and reports directly to the DAO. The ERC is not a substitute for the informal resolution process described in Section 8; rather, it is the formal body that renders final determinations on matters brought before it.

**6.2 Committee Composition.** The ERC shall be composed of the following voting members:

(a) The Director of the DAO, or designee, who shall serve as Chair;
(b) One (1) representative from the Office of Special Education;
(c) One (1) representative from the Office of Student Services;
(d) One (1) representative from the Office of Human Resources;
(e) One (1) classroom teacher, appointed by the Superintendent;
(f) One (1) parent or guardian of a student with a disability, appointed by the Superintendent; and
(g) One (1) student, appointed by the Superintendent, when the matter involves a student at the high school level.

The Chair may invite non-voting participants as needed, including but not limited to building principals, school nurses, counselors, or outside evaluators. Non-voting participants shall not be counted toward a quorum.

**6.3 Quorum and Voting.** A quorum shall consist of a majority of the voting members, provided that the Chair or designee is present. Decisions shall be made by a majority vote of the voting members present. In the event of a tie, the Chair shall cast the deciding vote. A member who has a conflict of interest in a matter shall recuse themselves and shall not count toward quorum for that matter.

**6.4 Meeting Frequency and Schedule.** The Eligibility Review Committee has_meeting_frequency monthly. Record status: current. The ERC shall meet at least once each calendar month, on a date and time determined by the Chair. The current meeting schedule is published on the DAO website and posted in the DAO office. Additional special meetings may be called by the Chair as needed to address urgent matters, including but not limited to interim accommodations pending a full review. Meetings shall be held in a location accessible to individuals with disabilities, and shall be conducted in accordance with the District's policies on open meetings. Minutes of each meeting shall be recorded and maintained in the DAO office.

**6.5 Matters Brought Before the ERC.** The ERC shall review the following matters:

(a) Appeals of eligibility determinations made by DAO staff;
(b) Appeals of accommodation decisions, including denials, modifications, or terminations;
(c) Requests for reconsideration of a determination due to a material change in circumstances;
(d) Disputes regarding the adequacy or reasonableness of documentation;
(e) Grievances involving the implementation of an approved accommodation plan; and
(f) Any other matter referred to the ERC by the DAO Director.

**6.6 Referral and Scheduling.** A request for ERC review must be submitted in writing to the DAO office using the Request for Eligibility Review form (Form DAO-7). The DAO shall acknowledge receipt of the request within five (5) business days. The Chair shall schedule the matter for the next available ERC meeting, provided that the request is received at least ten (10) business days before the meeting date. If the request is received fewer than ten (10) business days before a meeting, the matter shall be scheduled for the following meeting unless the Chair determines that an expedited review is warranted.

**6.7 Review Process.** In reviewing a matter, the ERC shall:

**7.5 Processing of Appeals.** Upon receipt of a first or second level appeal, the DAO office shall:

(a) Acknowledge receipt of the appeal in writing within five (5) business days;

(b) Assign the appeal to the appropriate reviewer (the DAO Director for first level appeals, or the Eligibility Review Committee for second level appeals);

(c) Compile the administrative record, which shall include all documents, evaluations, and correspondence considered in the initial determination;

(d) Provide the individual with a copy of the administrative record not less than ten (10) calendar days prior to the date of the review, unless the individual waives this right in writing.

**7.6 Review by the DAO Director (First Level Appeal).** The DAO Director shall review the administrative record and may, in the Director's discretion, conduct an interview with the individual or request additional information. The Director shall issue a written decision within thirty (30) calendar days of receipt of the appeal. The decision shall include a summary of the evidence considered, the basis for the decision, and notice of the right to file a second level appeal.

**7.7 Review by the Eligibility Review Committee (Second Level Appeal).** The ERC shall consist of three members appointed by the DAO Director, none of whom shall have participated in the initial determination or the first level appeal. The ERC shall review the administrative record and the DAO Director's decision. The ERC may, in its discretion, hold a hearing at which the individual may present information and question witnesses. The ERC shall issue a written decision within forty-five (45) calendar days of the date the appeal is referred to the ERC. The decision shall be final and shall not be subject to further internal appeal within the District, except as provided in Section 7.8 below regarding material change in circumstances.

**7.8 Material Change in Circumstances.** An individual whose application for an accommodation has been denied, or whose accommodation has been modified or revoked, may file a new application at any time if there is a material change in circumstances. A material change in circumstances includes, but is not limited to:

(a) A significant change in the individual's disability-related condition;

(b) A change in the physical layout, configuration, or accessibility of the building or facility in which the individual works, attends school, or receives services;

(c) The availability of new assistive technology or accommodation strategies that were not previously considered.

A new application filed under this Section shall be processed in accordance with Section 4 of this Policy Manual and shall not be considered an appeal of the prior determination.

**Section 8: Responsibilities of School Principals and Facilities Staff.**

**8.1 General Responsibilities.** Building principals and the Facilities and Maintenance Department share responsibility for implementing accessibility accommodations in accordance with this Policy Manual and any applicable federal, state, or local law. These responsibilities are described in this Section.

**8.2 Responsibilities of School Principals.** Each school principal, or the principal's designee, shall:

(a) Designate a staff member to serve as the school's Accessibility Coordinator. The name and contact information of the Accessibility Coordinator shall be posted in a conspicuous location in the school's main office and shall be provided to the DAO office annually by September 15.
